# cazalla.net workspace on MarkText

This document completes the roadmap notes. Each numbered section starts with the original notes, unchanged. The design follows. Open questions close each section.

## Scope and conventions

The target is the desktop app in `packages/desktop`. The website package is out of scope.

Feature code lives under `cazalla/` at the repository root. The alias `@cazalla` resolves to it in Vite, Vitest and TypeScript. Upstream files are changed only where a hook is unavoidable, by one to three lines each. `cazalla/README.md` lists the hooks that exist today.

The content folder is the preference `defaultDirectoryToOpen`. It plays the role of an Obsidian vault. All paths below are relative to it unless stated.

All feature logic runs in the renderer through the preload bridge `window.fileUtils` and `window.path`. The main process receives only menu and context-menu hooks. PDF generation runs in the renderer too: pdf-lib is pure JavaScript and `fileUtils.writeFile` accepts a `Uint8Array`.

New dependencies of `packages/desktop`:

| Package | Use |
| --- | --- |
| `yaml` 2.x | parse and serialize frontmatter; the Obsidian plugin already uses it |
| `@sindresorhus/slugify` | slugs; same algorithm as the Obsidian vault, so existing slugs keep matching; ESM only, renderer only |
| `lucide-vue-next` | icons; Lucide is the set Obsidian uses |
| `vue-draggable-plus` | SortableJS wrapper for the kanban columns |
| `pdf-lib` 1.17.1 | the ported invoice and report renderers |

Shared module `cazalla/renderer/core/`:

- `frontmatter.ts`: `split(markdown)` returns the fenced YAML block and the body; `parse(markdown)` returns an object; `serialize(fields, body)` writes a block with `yaml.stringify`; `updateFields(markdown, patch)` rewrites named keys and keeps the body. This generalizes `touchModified` in `cazalla/renderer/dailyNotes/util.ts`. The Obsidian plugin builds frontmatter with string templates and breaks on titles with `:`; serialization through `yaml` removes that class of error.
- `slug.ts`: wraps `@sindresorhus/slugify`.
- `time.ts`: `isoNow()` returns `toISOString()` (UTC, `Z`), used for notes, tasks and metadata as in the samples below; `localStamp()` returns the local time with offset, used by daily notes.
- `fs.ts`: typed wrappers over `window.fileUtils` and a recursive listing of `.md` files.
- `i18n.ts`: one registration of all cazalla locale files into vue-i18n; the daily-notes registration moves here.
- `contentFolder.ts`: a computed over `preferencesStore.defaultDirectoryToOpen`, shared by every store.

Frontmatter keys are written in the order shown in the samples. Timestamps in notes, tasks and metadata are UTC ISO strings. Daily notes keep their local-offset format; this difference is inherited from the Obsidian vault and is kept so existing files parse unchanged.

Implementation order: 2, 3, 6, 4, 5, 8, 9, then 7. Items 3 to 9 depend on 2. Item 6 depends on 3. Item 4 depends on 6. Items 5 and 8 depend on 4. Item 9 depends on 4. Item 7 is independent and comes last because it is the largest. Items 1 to 9 were implemented on 2026-10-23 and 2026-10-24.

Second round, added 2026-10-24: 10, 15, 12, 11, 14, then 13. Item 10 is fixes. Item 15 (explorer) comes early because items 11 and 12 open their files from it. Item 13 is the largest and last.

Third round, added 2026-10-24: the second "details" section, then 13, 17, 16, and search last. Item 16 depends on search for its document list, and 17 shares its insertion mechanism.

Fourth round, added 2026-10-24: 19 (details), then 20 (lazygit in an xterm.js pane; 18 superseded).

# 1 calendar and daily notes.

Once a base dir is selected
daily notes live under _daily
a calendar is shown in a right panel in the top sub-panel
daily notes are edited in lower sub-panel of the right panel

frontmatter:

title: "2026-10-24"
slug: 2026-10-24
created: 2026-10-24T00:12:56+02:00
modified: 2026-10-24T00:12:56+02:00
category: daily
tags: []

## Status

Implemented. The design is in `cazalla/README.md`. The panel toggles from View › Daily Notes, `Ctrl+Shift+A`, or the command palette. Notes live at `_daily/YYYY/MM/YYYY-MM-DD.md`.

Follow-up from this roadmap: the daily-notes `util.ts` frontmatter helpers migrate to `cazalla/renderer/core/frontmatter.ts` when item 2 lands. Behaviour does not change.

# 2 metadata

in the root dir there is a directory _metadata/
with 3 subdirectories people/ organizations/ projects.
these contain md docs that only have yaml frontmatter.

people have frontmatter:

name: Some Name
slug: some-name
organization: <organization-slug>
email:
phone:

organizations have common frontmatter:

name:
slug:
leader: <person-slug>

for organizations that can invoice:

price_hour: 55
invoice_number: 0026
iban: ES62...
swift: INGDESMMXXX
payment_details: 30 days
invoice_details: []

for organizations that can be invoiced:

name:
slug:
leader: <person-slug>
phone:
billing:
  - <project-slug>
  - ...
invoice_details: []

projects have frontmatter:

name:
slug:
organization: <organization-slug>
leader: <person-slug>
service:
path: path/to/project/files/from/root

All this metadata is loaded at startup and kept for use in other tasks,
look in ~/cazalla/cazalla-net.plugin/main.ts and
~/cazalla/cazalla-net.plugin/src/metadata.ts,
we will want to import some functionality from that repo

## File format

Each metadata file is a markdown document whose content is one fenced `---` YAML block and an empty body. MarkText renders it as a normal document. The loader parses only the block.

The Obsidian plugin reads `Metadata/companies`, `Metadata/projects` and `Metadata/people` as plain YAML without fences (`src/metadata.ts:150-169`). The new layout renames `companies` to `organizations`, lowercases the directory with a leading underscore, and adds fences. Existing vault files need a one-time migration: add fences, move directories, rename the key `company` to `organization`. The migration is a script under `cazalla/scripts/`, run by hand.

## Data model

Types port from `src/metadata.ts:5-118` with these changes: `Company` becomes `Organization`; `Person.company: string[]` becomes `Person.organization: string`; `Project.company: string[]` becomes `Project.organization: string`; `Person` gains `phone`; `Organization` gains `phone`. The invoicing and invoiced field groups are optional on `Organization`. Each record also carries `file`, the absolute path it was read from.

An organization can invoice when `iban` is set. An organization can be invoiced when `billing` is a non-empty list.

## Store

`cazalla/renderer/metadata/store.ts` is a Pinia setup store with `people`, `organizations` and `projects` as maps keyed by slug, plus `loaded` and `errors`.

`load()` lists `_metadata/people`, `_metadata/organizations` and `_metadata/projects`, reads each `.md`, parses the frontmatter, and fills the maps. A record without `slug` gets the file stem as slug. A duplicate slug is kept once and reported through `notice.notify`. A file that fails to parse is reported and skipped.

The trigger is `watch(contentFolder, load, { immediate: true })`, the pattern already used in `cazalla/renderer/dailyNotes/store.ts`. The store reloads after each of its own writes. When the content folder is also an open sidebar root, the store listens to the `mt::update-object-tree` events the main watcher already sends and reloads when a changed path is under `_metadata/`. There is no separate watcher.

Consumers read the maps directly. Dialog selects list `projects`, `organizations` and `people` by name, sorted.

The plugin loads once and never invalidates (`src/metadata.ts:136-147`). The store above replaces that behaviour with reload on change.

## Open questions

- Which organization is "us" for invoices. The plugin hardcodes the slug `cazalla-net` (`src/metadata.ts:233`). Proposed convention: the single organization with `iban` set; when several qualify, the invoice dialog asks.
- Whether `_metadata/` should be hidden from the sidebar tree. Proposed: no; the files are editable documents.

# 3 side bar with clickable icons

if no icon library present select one - the one in obsidian is good
  in tasks below we will be adding icons to the bar.

  To start with, we want icons to create a person, a project, or a company.
  clicking on them will open a dialog with the pertinent fields,
  and saving the dialogs will create the pertinent files.

## Icon library

The desktop package ships `@element-plus/icons-vue`. Obsidian uses Lucide. `lucide-vue-next` is added for the activity bar; it tree-shakes to the icons imported.

## Activity bar

`cazalla/renderer/activityBar/ActivityBar.vue` is a 40 px vertical strip placed left of the sidebar. It is always visible and does not depend on the sidebar toggle. Each entry is a lucide icon inside the upstream `IconTooltip` component with a translated title. Clicking an entry emits a bus event named `cazalla:<action>`.

Hook: one tag in `pages/app.vue` before `<side-bar>`. The bar's width is added to the width the cazalla store already reports to `editorWithTabs/index.vue`, so no further upstream change is needed. The daily-notes store's `effectiveWidth` moves to a small cazalla layout store that both the panel and the bar feed.

Entries, in order, as the roadmap grows: New Person, New Organization, New Project (this item); New Note, New Task (item 4); Kanban (item 5); Effort Report, Invoice (item 9).

## Dialog framework

`cazalla/renderer/dialogs/EntityDialog.vue` wraps `el-dialog` and `el-form`. It is mounted once from `ActivityBar.vue`, so no upstream mount is needed. It opens on the bus event `cazalla:dialog` with a spec:

- `title`: translated heading.
- `fields`: a list of `{ key, label, type, required, options, default }` where `type` is `text`, `textarea`, `number`, `date` or `select`; `options` for a select is a function returning `{ value, label }[]`, usually from the metadata store.
- `submit(values)`: an async function; it returns an error string to show, or nothing on success.

Validation is by hand, as in the upstream dialogs: required fields, slug uniqueness against the metadata store, and target path existence before writing. Errors show inline under the field. The dialog closes on success.

The upstream pattern this follows is `components/rename/index.vue` (bus-opened `el-dialog`, focus on open, Enter to confirm).

## Entity dialogs

`cazalla/renderer/metadata/dialogs.ts` defines the three specs and their submit functions.

New Person: name, organization (select), email, phone. Writes `_metadata/people/<slug>.md`.

New Organization: name, leader (select of people), phone; a checkbox "can invoice" revealing price_hour, invoice_number, iban, swift, payment_details, invoice_details (textarea, one line per entry); a checkbox "can be invoiced" revealing billing (multi-select of projects) and invoice_details. Writes `_metadata/organizations/<slug>.md`.

New Project: see item 6.

Each submit serializes the frontmatter with `yaml`, writes the file, and calls `metadata.load()`. The slug is derived from the name and shown read-only in the dialog.

The plugin has no dialogs for people, companies or projects; these are new. Its task and note dialogs (`main.ts:932-1080`) are the model for the fields.

## Open questions

- Whether the activity bar replaces the sidebar footer buttons (settings, open folder) or leaves them. Proposed: leave them.

# 4 new categories of md docs: notes and tasks

frontmatter for notes:

title: Some Note
slug: some-note
tags:
date: 2026-10-22T15:06:42.215Z
modified: 2026-10-22T15:06:42.215Z
category: note
project: <project-slug>

frontmatter for tasks:

title: Some Task
slug: some-task
tags: []
date: 2026-10-22T13:09:21.904Z
modified: 2026-10-22T13:09:21.904Z
category: task
project: <project-slug>
path:
status: new
due: 2026-11-22T14:09:21.904Z
effort: []
subtasks: []

the left icon bar has 2 icons, for "New Note" and "New Task"
each of them opens a dialog, asking for title, project (choosing from the project metadata)
(due in the case of task)
and creates the file under the project path, either under notes/ or tasks/

## Dialogs

New Note: title, project (select). Writes `<project.path>/notes/<slug>.md` with the note frontmatter and an empty body. `tags` is written as `[]`.

New Task: title, project (select), due (date, default today plus one month, the plugin's rule at `main.ts:633`). Writes `<project.path>/tasks/<slug>.md` with the task frontmatter. `path` is `/` for a top-level task. `status` is `new`. `effort` and `subtasks` are `[]`.

Both refuse an existing path. Both open the new file as a tab through `mt::open-file`, the channel the sidebar uses. The sidebar tree updates by itself through the main-process watcher when the project folder is under an open root.

## Task index

`cazalla/renderer/tasks/store.ts` indexes every task. For each project it lists `tasks/**/*.md` and `archived/**/*.md`, parses the frontmatter, and keeps `{ file, title, slug, project, path, status, due, effort, subtasks }`. It loads when metadata loads, reloads on tree events under a project path, and after its own writes.

The index serves the kanban (item 5), subtasks (item 8) and reports (item 9). The plugin instead scans the whole vault through Obsidian's metadata cache (`src/metadata.ts:193-210`); the index replaces that.

Writes to a task go through `frontmatter.updateFields`, which also sets `modified`.

## Effort entries

`effort` is a list of one-key maps, `- 2026-10-22: 2.5`, as the plugin reads them (`src/metadata.ts:170-212`). Entering effort is done by editing the frontmatter in the editor. No dialog is planned in this roadmap.

## Open questions

- Whether `status` values other than the six column names should be tolerated in the index. Proposed: unknown values show in `new` and are reported once.

# 5 kanban board

Investigate which kanban board might be
appropriate to integrate here.

will have columns:

continuous, new, pending, wip, done, archived

## Investigation

Candidates found: SVAR Vue Kanban (`@svar-ui/vue-kanban`), a complete Vue 3 board with TypeScript and themes; Syncfusion Vue Kanban, commercial; kanvue and drag-and-drop-vue, small and little maintained; vue-kanban (BrockReece), Vue 2 era, dragula based. Building blocks: `vue-draggable-plus` and `vue-draggable-next`, both SortableJS wrappers.

Decision: build the board on `vue-draggable-plus`. The board has six fixed columns, cards with a title and a due date, and one behaviour on drop. A full kanban component brings its own data model and styling for little gain, and SVAR's licence would need checking. The desktop package already depends on `dragula`, but it is used for tab ordering only and has no Vue 3 binding; SortableJS is the common choice for Vue 3 lists.

## Data

There is no board file. The board is a view over the task index. A column holds the tasks whose `status` equals the column name. The plugin used Obsidian Kanban markdown files and never synced status (`main.ts:326-437`); that format is dropped.

## View

`cazalla/renderer/kanban/KanbanView.vue` fills the main editor area. Hook: one tag in `pages/app.vue` beside `<editor-with-tabs>`, shown when a cazalla layout flag `mainView === 'kanban'` is set; the editor is hidden with `v-show` so its state survives.

The header has a project select, from the metadata store, and a close button. The default project is the `project` of the current file when it has one. Columns are `continuous`, `new`, `pending`, `wip`, `done`, `archived`, in that order. A card shows the title, the due date, and a subtask count. Clicking a card opens the task through `mt::open-file`. Cards of subtasks are indented under their parent's slug label.

The view opens from the activity bar icon and from a sidebar context-menu entry "Kanban" on a project folder. The sidebar hook is one entry in `contextMenu/sideBar/index.ts` and a handler for the bus event it emits.

## Behaviour

Dropping a card in a column other than `archived` writes `status: <column>` and `modified` through `frontmatter.updateFields`. The index refreshes and the card stays where it was dropped.

Dropping a card in `archived` runs the archive procedure of item 8. A task that still has subtasks is refused with a notice and the card snaps back.

`continuous` is a status for tasks that never close, such as support contracts. It behaves like any other non-archived column.

## Open questions

- Whether cards should be reorderable inside a column and the order persisted. Proposed: no; order is by due date.

# 6 projects

a project has a path from the root under projects/
it has subfolders archived/, notes/, and tasks/,
and has a kanban board.

The icon for "New Project" mentioned in 3
should create all the relevant directories and files.

## Dialog

New Project: name, organization (select), leader (select), service (text), path (text, default `projects/<slug>`).

Submit creates `<path>/`, `<path>/archived/`, `<path>/notes/` and `<path>/tasks/` with `fileUtils.ensureDir`, then writes `_metadata/projects/<slug>.md` with the project frontmatter, then reloads metadata. The sidebar tree shows directories, so empty folders are visible without placeholder files.

A project has no board file. Its board is the kanban view filtered by its slug.

The plugin stores `path` with a trailing slash and resolves `Tasks/` with a capital T (`main.ts:615-663`). The new layout uses lowercase `tasks/`, `notes/`, `archived/` and no trailing slash. The migration script of item 2 renames the directories.

## Open questions

- Whether a project's `path` may point outside `projects/`. Proposed: allowed, since `path` is explicit in the frontmatter.

# 7 split panels

it should be possible to split the editing/viewing main panel
both vertically and horizontally,
and also close a subpanel,
through entries in the contextual menu of the main panel(s)
"split horiz" "split vert" "Close panel".

## Finding

The upstream editor is single-pane by construction. The store holds one `currentFile` (`store/editor.ts:140-149`). The editor component swaps documents through untargeted bus events (`editor.vue:1831-1873`). Menu actions, find and replace, and the IPC context-menu handlers address "the" editor. `window.DIRNAME` is one value. Full multi-pane support means rewriting `editor.vue` and the editor store, which conflicts with the minimal-hook rule.

## Design

The upstream editor stays the primary pane. It keeps tabs, menus, find and replace, and source-code mode.

Secondary panes are instances of `cazalla/renderer/panes/FilePane.vue`, a generalization of `cazalla/renderer/dailyNotes/NoteEditor.vue`. A file pane owns one Muya instance bound to an absolute path. It reads the file on open, autosaves with a one-second debounce through `fileUtils.writeFile`, flushes on blur and close, and reloads when a tree `change` event names its path and it has no unsaved edits. The daily-notes editor becomes a file pane with a fixed path.

`cazalla/renderer/panes/store.ts` holds the layout: a binary tree whose leaves are either `primary` or `{ file }`, whose inner nodes have a direction and a split ratio. The tree is persisted per window in `localStorage`. `cazalla/renderer/panes/PaneLayout.vue` renders the tree recursively with draggable dividers, the divider logic coming from the daily-notes panel.

Hook: `pages/app.vue` wraps `<editor-with-tabs>` in `<pane-layout>`, which renders the primary slot where the tree says. That is one tag pair.

## Context-menu entries

The editor's right-click menu is built in the main process (`src/main/contextMenu/editor/index.ts`, items in `menuItems.ts`). `cazalla/main/contextMenu.ts` exports factories for "Split horizontally", "Split vertically" and "Close panel". Each sends `mt::cm-cazalla-pane` with an action name to the focused window. Hook: one spread line in `getContextItems()`. The panes store listens for the channel and acts on the focused pane. Labels come from the cazalla locale files, as the Daily Notes menu label does.

Split creates a sibling file pane showing the file of the focused pane. Close removes the focused pane; the primary pane cannot be closed. A sidebar entry "Open in split" opens a tree file in a new file pane; it is one entry in the sidebar context menu.

Focus tracking: a pane records itself as focused on `mousedown` and on Muya `focus`.

## Limitations

Secondary panes have no tabs. Menu formatting shortcuts, find and replace, and the outline act on the primary pane. Secondary panes have no source-code mode. Muya UI plugins are registered process-wide, so image actions in a secondary pane resolve against the primary pane's file.

## Alternative recorded

A full pane model: per-pane `fileId` in the editor store, `currentFile` derived from the focused pane, bus payloads carrying a `paneId`, `editor.vue` taking a `fileId` prop. It removes the limitations above and touches most of `editor.vue` and the editor store. It is not chosen for this roadmap.

## Open questions

- Whether a secondary pane should be able to show the kanban view. Proposed: later.

# 8 subtasks

Any task can have subtasks.
On the contextual menu of a task in main panel
there is an entry "add subtask".
clicking it opens a dialog asking for a title.
saving it:
- at the same level as the task, create a subdir with name the slug of the task if it does not exist
- create a subtask within the created subdir
- same project as task
- as path of the subtask, set the path of the task slash the slug of the task
- add an entry in the tasks' frontmatter, subtasks, with value the path of the subtask slash the slug of the filename of the subtag
- add a card in the kanban board with a link to the subtask, in column new

When a card in a kanban boar is moved to archived column,
the corresponding subtask is moved to the archived/ dir of the project,
with the same subpath as set in the subtasks frontmatter of the parent task,
its entry is removed from the parent subtasks frontmatter,
if there are no more subtasks, the parent task directory is removed,
and the card is removed from the board.

## Context-menu entry

"Add subtask" is a fourth factory in `cazalla/main/contextMenu.ts`, sending `mt::cm-cazalla-subtask`. It uses the same spread-line hook as item 7. The main process cannot see the document, so the entry always shows. The renderer handler checks that the current file's frontmatter has `category: task`; otherwise it shows a notice and stops.

## Create procedure

Given parent task file `<dir>/<parent-slug>.md` with `path: P` (where `P` is `/` for a top-level task):

1. Ask for a title in the dialog. Derive `<slug>`.
2. Ensure directory `<dir>/<parent-slug>/`.
3. Write `<dir>/<parent-slug>/<slug>.md` with the task frontmatter, `project` copied from the parent, `path: P/<parent-slug>` normalized to a single leading slash, `status: new`, `due` default plus one month.
4. Append `P/<parent-slug>/<slug>` to the parent's `subtasks` through `updateFields`.
5. Refresh the task index. The card appears in `new` because the board derives from the index.

The plugin's version (`main.ts:666-750`) also inserts a wiki link into the parent body and a card into a kanban file. Both are dropped: the board is derived, and the `subtasks` list is the link.

## Archive procedure

Used by the kanban `archived` drop, for subtasks and top-level tasks alike. Given task file `F` in project `R` with `path: P` and slug `S`:

1. If the task's `subtasks` is non-empty, refuse with a notice.
2. Compute the target `R/archived/P/S.md`, ensure its directory, move `F` there with `fileUtils.move`.
3. Set `status: archived` and `modified` in the moved file.
4. If `P` is not `/`, find the parent task: its slug is the last segment of `P`, its file is the sibling of the directory `F` was in. Remove `P/S` from its `subtasks`.
5. If the directory `F` was in is now empty, remove it.
6. Refresh the index. The card disappears because the task is no longer in a column.

The plugin's archive (`main.ts:385-437`) moves `path` to `archived-path` and rewrites `path` as `/archived/`. The new procedure keeps `path` unchanged, since the archived location already encodes it.

## Open questions

- Whether a parent with subtasks may be archived together with them. Proposed above: refuse; archive the children first.
- Whether `effort` on a subtask should count toward the parent in reports. Proposed: reports sum by `project`, so both count once.

# 9 effort reports and invoices.

add 2 icons to the left bar
and port to them the behaviour of generating effort reports and invoices
from ~/cazalla/cazalla-net.plugin/

## Port

`src/invoice.ts` (200 lines) is pure code over pdf-lib and Node `fs`; it moves to `cazalla/renderer/billing/invoicePdf.ts` with `fs.writeFileSync` replaced by returning the bytes. `generateReport` (`main.ts:12-88`) is pure in practice and moves to `cazalla/renderer/billing/reportPdf.ts` the same way. The caller writes the bytes with `fileUtils.writeFile(path, bytes)`.

The data functions `getProjectData` and `getInvoiceData` (`src/metadata.ts:170-312`) are rewritten over the task index and the metadata store in `cazalla/renderer/billing/data.ts`. Effort entries are `{ 'YYYY-MM-DD': hours }`; the date range is inclusive, the end date pushed to 23:59, as in the plugin.

Two fixes carried into the port: the `forEach(async …)` at `src/metadata.ts:193` becomes a plain loop; month names in paths become numeric so the path does not depend on the system locale.

## Dialogs

Effort Report: project (select), start date, end date (defaults: first and last day of the previous month). Output `_invoices/YYYY/MM/report-<project>-YYYY-MM.pdf`.

Invoice: organization (select of organizations with `billing`), start date, end date. One service line per project in the organization's `billing`: description `<project.service> <month>`, quantity the summed hours, unit price our `price_hour`, VAT as in the plugin. Our details come from our organization record (see item 2, open question). Output `_invoices/YYYY/MM/invoice-<org>-YYYY-MM-<nnnn>.pdf`.

The invoice number is `<year><nnnn>` with `nnnn` zero-padded. The counter is `invoice_number` in our organization file. It is incremented through `updateFields` after the PDF is written. The plugin increments before writing (`src/metadata.ts:213-230`), which skips a number when the write fails.

Both dialogs refuse to overwrite an existing output file. On success a notice shows the written path.

## Open questions

- Whether the invoice layout should move from hardcoded coordinates to a template. Proposed: port as is; a template is a later item.
- Currency and VAT rate are constants in the plugin. Proposed: keep constants in `cazalla/renderer/billing/config.ts` until a second client needs different ones.


# 10 details so far

## upper corners

Both the calendar and the left hand toolbar overlap with the window controls at the top - burger menu and window controls.

## org md

remove services from organization metadata

## tasks and kanban

add `kanban`key to task frontmatter with value true/false so if false it does not show in kanban board

## project structure

When creating projects, the corresponding folders are provided with a further subfolder `assets`.
invoices and effort repoprts are kept in the corresponding assets/invoices/<year>/<month>/

## Design

### Upper corners

The upstream title bar is `position: fixed` across the whole window (`titleBar/index.vue`, `.title-bar`). The burger menu sits at the top left and the window controls at the top right, above whatever column is there. The upstream sidebar leaves room with `padding-top: 32px`. The activity bar and the daily-notes panel get the same `padding-top: var(--titleBarHeight)`, and their headers stop declaring `-webkit-app-region: drag`, which the fixed title bar already provides.

### Organization metadata

Organizations never carried `service`; the key belongs to projects only. The organization dialog and `Organization` type have no such field. Nothing to change.

### Folders

A project folder gets a fourth subfolder, `assets/`. An organization gets a folder `organizations/<slug>/` with `notes/` and `assets/`. The slug is used, as for projects, because names such as `cazalla.net` make poor path segments. The organization record gains no new key; its folder is derived from the slug. The vault migration script creates the missing folders for existing projects and organizations.

### Invoices and effort reports

Decision of 2026-10-24, superseding the note above: the PDFs go to the organization's assets, not the project's. An invoice is written to `organizations/<client slug>/assets/invoices/<year>/<month>/`. An effort report is written to the same path under the project's organization; a project without `organization` cannot produce a report, and the dialog says so. `_invoices/` at the root is no longer used.

### Kanban key

Tasks and subtasks are created with `kanban: true`. The index reads the key; absent means `true`. The board shows only tasks whose `kanban` is not `false`. A task edited to `kanban: false` disappears from the board on the next index refresh.

Code: `metadata/create.ts` (organization folder, project `assets/`), `tasks/create.ts` and `tasks/subtasks.ts` (`kanban: true`), `tasks/store.ts` and `kanban/KanbanView.vue` (filter), `billing/data.ts` and `billing/dialogs.ts` (output path), `activityBar/ActivityBar.vue` and `dailyNotes/Panel.vue` (padding), `scripts/migrate-vault.ts`.

# 11 excalidraw

We want an integration with excalidraw.
The left hand side bar will include an icon to add a excalidraw drawing.
it will show dialog asking for name and project.
it will be kept in assets/drawings/<year>/<month>/.

## Design

Decision of 2026-10-24: the official React component. `@excalidraw/excalidraw` needs `react` and `react-dom`; both are added to the desktop package and used only by the drawing pane. The team has retired its own Electron wrapper in favour of the web app, so the component is the supported route for embedding.

`cazalla/renderer/drawings/DrawingPane.vue` is a pane leaf like `FilePane`: it mounts a React root into a `div`, renders `<Excalidraw initialData={…} onChange={…}>`, and autosaves the scene as `.excalidraw` JSON (elements, appState subset, files) one second after the last change. It unmounts the React root on close. The pane tree gains a leaf kind `drawing` with a file path; `PaneLayout.vue` renders it.

The activity bar gets a "New drawing" entry. The dialog asks for name and project and writes `<project path>/assets/drawings/<year>/<month>/<slug>.excalidraw` with an empty scene, then opens it in a split pane. Existing drawings open from the explorer (item 15), which shows all files, and from links in documents whose target ends in `.excalidraw`.

Export: the pane header has "Export PNG" and "Export SVG", using the package's `exportToBlob` and `exportToSvg`, written next to the drawing. A markdown document embeds the exported image with a normal image link.

Open: whether a drawing is referenced from its project's notes automatically. Proposed: no; the user links the exported image.

# 12 pdfjs

Use pdfjs to visualize PDFs

## Design

`vue-pdf-embed` wraps pdf.js for Vue 3 with no peer dependencies and is current with pdf.js 5. It is added to the desktop package. `cazalla/renderer/pdf/PdfPane.vue` is a pane leaf (`kind: 'pdf'`) that renders every page in a scrolling column with zoom controls and a page counter; the file is read through the bridge as bytes and handed to the component as binary data.

Entry points: clicking a `.pdf` file in the explorer (item 15), links in documents that end in `.pdf`, and the notice after generating a report or an invoice, which gets an "Open" action that opens the PDF in a split pane.

Link interception: Muya reports link clicks to the editor through `jumpClick`; the upstream editor resolves them with `FORMAT_LINK_CLICK`. A cazalla hook of one line in that store action checks the extension and routes `.pdf` and `.excalidraw` targets to the pane store instead of the system opener. The exact hook is settled when the item starts.

Open: whether the main viewer should also handle images and other binaries. Proposed: later; the explorer opens unknown types with the system handler.

# 13 websites

New dir `sites/` under _metadata/.
New entry in the left tool bar, "New Site",
that will add a new file to sites/, with frontmatter:

name: Some Name
slug: some-name
site-url: https://some.url
path: <path to site files relative to root dir>
ssh-host: <Hetzner server hostname or IP>
ssh-port: <default 22>
ssh-user: <default deploy>
ssh-target-dir: <default /var/www/site>

this will also create a folder in path,
with subdirs assets/ and pages/

then we have a further entry in the left tool bar,
"New page" to add pages to websites.

a webpage is a md doc with frontmatter:

title: Some Webpage
slug: some-webpage
tags:
date: 2026-09-06T15:37:24.357Z
modified: 2026-10-24T10:04:57.261Z
category: webpage
status: draft
project: <some-project-slug>
order: <number of sibling pages>
path: /

So the dialog asks for title and project
and creates doc in the path of the project /pages/

Then when editing a webpage, the contextual menu
has an entry "add sub-webpage"
that will ask for title,
and behave like "add subtask"
minus the kanban and archive stuff.

finally adapt cazalla/cazalla-net-site.plugin/
to have one further icon in the left bar,
"publish site", that will ask for a site name
and update the build environemnt if needed and build and publish the site.

## Design

### What the Obsidian plugin does today

`cazalla-net-site.plugin` serves one site. Settings hold `vaultContentDir`, `sshHost`, `sshPort`, `sshUser`, `sshTargetDir` and `siteUrl`. The generator is Pelican, shipped inside the plugin directory under `pelican/` with a theme, a `vault_metadata` plugin, a Makefile and `requirements.txt`. Initialization creates a Python venv and symlinks the content directory as `pelican/content`. Building runs `make draft` or `make publish`; publishing runs `make rsync_upload`, which is `rsync -e "ssh -p $SSH_PORT" -P -rvzc --delete output/ $SSH_USER@$SSH_HOST:$SSH_TARGET_DIR`. Settings reach the Makefile as environment variables. Credentials are the user's ssh keys and `~/.ssh/config`. Page URLs derive from file location; `status: public` gates publication; `order` sorts siblings; the sub-page dialog creates a sibling directory named after the page.

### Site records

`_metadata/sites/<slug>.md` with the frontmatter in the note above. `path` is the site folder relative to the content folder. The metadata store gains a fourth kind, `sites`, and the New site dialog (activity bar) writes the record and creates `<path>/assets/` and `<path>/pages/`. Defaults: `ssh-port` 22, `ssh-user` deploy, `ssh-target-dir` /var/www/site.

### Webpages

New page dialog: title and site (the note says project; the page lives under a site's `pages/`, so the select lists sites, and `project` in the frontmatter is the site slug). The file is `<site path>/pages/<slug>.md` with the frontmatter in the note; `order` is the number of sibling `.md` files in the target folder; `path` is `/`. The context-menu entry "Add sub-webpage" works like "Add subtask" without kanban and archive: a folder named after the parent page, a page inside it with `path` extended by the parent slug and `order` from its siblings. Pelican derives URLs from the file tree, so this layout maps directly to `<parent>/<child>/`.

### Publishing

`cazalla/renderer/sites/publish.ts` reproduces the plugin's shell flow through a main-process helper, because the renderer is sandboxed. `cazalla/main/sites.ts` registers one IPC handler, `mt::cazalla-run-site-command`, which spawns `make <target>` in the Pelican directory with the site's values as environment variables and streams stdout and stderr back to the renderer. The hook is one registration line in the main IPC setup.

The Pelican project (`pelican/` of the site plugin: Makefile, configs, theme, `plugins/vault_metadata`, `requirements.txt`) is copied into `cazalla/pelican/` in this repository and shipped as an extra resource of the app. At runtime it is copied to `<userData>/cazalla/pelican/<site slug>/` on first use. The Makefile's `INPUTDIR` becomes overridable so the content directory is `<site path>/pages` without a symlink.

The "Publish site" dialog asks for the site and shows a log panel. Steps: create the venv and install requirements when `venv/` is missing; `make publish`; `make rsync_upload`. Each step's exit code is checked; the log stays open on failure. Unix only, as the plugin.

Open: whether `make draft` with a local preview belongs in the first version. Proposed: a "Build draft" button that runs `make draft` and opens `output/index.html` in a pane is cheap once the log panel exists.

# 14 events

An event is a task that is due in the future or within the day.
in the left panel with the file explorer,
in the same way that there is a magnifier icon that opens up a search interface,
we want a further icon that opens an event interface.
4 section from top to bottom, each with a list:
- today: tasks due for today - even if for a past time
- tomorrow
- this week
- the rest
each task in the event list is shown as a link that
opens the task in the main panel.
all lists are ordered from nearer in time up.

## Design

An event is a task with a `due` date. The sidebar gains a third column view, `events`, beside `files` and `search`. Upstream normalizes the column to `files` or `search` in `store/layout.ts`; the hook adds `events` there and one `v-else-if` in `sideBar/index.vue`, plus a calendar-clock icon in the sidebar header next to the magnifier. The view itself is `cazalla/renderer/events/EventsView.vue`.

Four sections, each a list sorted by `due` ascending: today, tomorrow, this week (from the day after tomorrow to the end of the ISO week, Monday-based, following the calendar's week start), and the rest. Overdue tasks are listed first under today, marked. Archived tasks and tasks with `kanban: false` are excluded; the second exclusion is open. Each entry shows the title, the project and the due time; clicking opens the task through `mt::open-file`. Sections with no entries show a dash. The view reads the task index and refreshes with it.

Open: whether done tasks should disappear from the lists. Proposed: yes; only `continuous`, `new`, `pending` and `wip` are events.

# 15 drag and drop to move files

I want to be able to move directories and files
by dragging and dropping in the left file explorer.
Any of these moves should keep all metadata
described above in sync, without corruption,
and also should update all links to moved content.

## Design

Decision of 2026-10-24: the upstream file tree is replaced by a cazalla explorer, and the explorer shows every file, not only markdown.

### Explorer

`cazalla/renderer/explorer/Explorer.vue` is built on Element Plus `el-tree`, already a dependency: lazy loading of directories through the bridge, every entry shown with a type icon (folder, markdown, pdf, excalidraw, image, other), `draggable` nodes, `allow-drop` restricted to directories (inner) and `node-drop` triggering a move. Hidden entries (`.git`, `node_modules`, the exclude patterns preference) are skipped. Clicking a markdown file opens it as a tab, a PDF or drawing opens in a pane, anything else with the system opener. The existing context-menu entries (new file, folder, rename, trash, copy path, kanban, open in split) keep working through the same bus events, with the active item set by the explorer. The upstream tree component stays in the repository and is no longer rendered; the hook is one `v-if` in `sideBar/index.vue` that renders the explorer instead of `<tree>`.

Refresh: the explorer reloads a directory after its own operations, on upstream `mt::update-object-tree` events for markdown files and folders, and on an explicit refresh button. Non-markdown changes made outside the app appear on refresh or when the folder is reopened; a cazalla watcher for all files is a follow-up.

### Move procedure

A move is one `fileUtils.move`, followed by:

1. Metadata: when a moved folder is or contains a project path, the project's `path` is rewritten; the same for sites. Tasks and notes keep no absolute paths, so moving them within the same project changes nothing in frontmatter. Moving a task between projects rewrites `project`. Moving a subtask out of its parent's folder removes it from the parent's `subtasks`; moving it in appends.
2. Links: every markdown file under the content folder is scanned for links and images whose target resolves to the moved path or inside it; relative targets are rewritten so they resolve to the new location. Files inside the moved folder have their outgoing relative links rewritten too. The scan is a simple regex over `](…)` targets and `[[…]]` wiki links; the rewrite uses `window.path.relative`.
3. Indexes: metadata, tasks and the explorer reload.

The procedure lives in `cazalla/renderer/explorer/move.ts` and is unit-tested over the in-memory file system used by the other specs. Moves that would overwrite an existing path are refused. A move is reported in a notice with the number of links rewritten.

Open: whether to offer undo. Proposed: not in the first version; the move is reversible by dragging back, and link rewriting is symmetric.

# 15 details so far

## project frontmatter

remove key services

## kanban board sink

when task cards move to "archived" column,
after all the file and link moves,
the card should disappear from the column
- perhaps with some funny animation.

## moving files warning

the file moves and renames by dragging in file explorer or in kanban or whatever
must never break anything
including links in frontmatter ut also links in content.

## two iconss in left tool bar

one for "Toggle left panel" another for "Toggle right panel".
both the top most.

## smaller frontmatter

2 pts smaller than content

## Design

Project records lose `service`; the invoice line description becomes the project name followed by the month. Existing files may keep the key; the loader ignores it.

The kanban `archived` column is a sink. It shows no cards. A card dropped there fades and shrinks for 300 ms while the archive procedure runs, then disappears; on failure it snaps back to its column.

Moves and renames go through one procedure (`explorer/move.ts`). It rewrites project `path`, task `project`, `path` and the parent's `subtasks`, and every relative link in content. Frontmatter values are not links except those listed, so no other frontmatter changes.

The activity bar gets two entries at the top: "Toggle left panel" (the sidebar, through the upstream layout toggle) and "Toggle right panel" (the daily notes panel). A separator follows them.

Frontmatter blocks render 2 pt smaller than the body: a global rule on `.mu-container pre.front-matter` in `cazalla/renderer/core/styles.css`, loaded once from the activity bar.

# 15 search

I want decent search.
not sure what marktext uses.
if needed, replace with full text indexing engine
that can deal with PDFs and other assets
can do fuzzy search

## Design

Upstream search is ripgrep over the sidebar roots: literal or regex, no ranking, no PDFs. It is replaced by a MiniSearch index, chosen for its size, fuzzy and prefix matching, and JSON serialization.

`cazalla/renderer/search/index.ts` builds one index per content folder with the fields `title`, `body`, `tags`, `category`, `project` and `path`. Documents: markdown files (frontmatter fields plus body text), PDFs (text extracted with pdf.js page by page) and drawings (text elements of the scene). Hidden folders are skipped. The index and a map of file mtimes are stored in `<content folder>/.cazalla/search-index.json`; on start the index loads and files whose mtime changed are re-indexed; the explorer's move procedure and the upstream tree events trigger incremental updates. A full rebuild is available from the view.

`cazalla/renderer/search/SearchView.vue` replaces the upstream sidebar search in the `search` column (one hook: the `v-else-if` branch in `sideBar/index.vue`). It has one input, fuzzy and prefix on by default, filters by category, and a result list with title, path and a highlighted snippet. Clicking opens the file in the editor or in a pane by type. Results are ranked by MiniSearch's score with a boost on title.

Open: whether to keep ripgrep as a "regex" mode. Proposed: no; regex users can still use the upstream search from the command palette if it stays reachable.

# 16 link dialog

when editing a document I double type "[" ("[[") i want a dialog box
with an optional label
and an href, that can either be typed in
or chosen from a list of recently created docs
that can be filtered in a text input,
searching in all docs by title.

## Design

Trigger: a keystroke listener on the editor container detects `[` typed right after `[` in the same text position. It is installed by a cazalla module from the activity bar and works for the primary editor and file panes.

Dialog: label (optional) and href. The href field is a filterable select over an index of documents by title, most recently created first, with free text allowed; a typed value is kept as written. The list comes from the search index of item 15 (titles and paths), so this item depends on it.

On accept the two `[` characters are removed and `[label](href)` is inserted at the cursor, with the href relative to the current document's folder when a vault document was chosen; the label defaults to the chosen title. The insertion API is settled by the exploration of Muya's cursor and text methods.

# 17 dates

an icon in left tool bar "insert date",
opens a dialog with
a checkbox date/datetime, default date,
and a date/datetime chooser, default now,
on accept inserts the date/time wherever the cursor is

## Design

An activity-bar entry "Insert date" opens a dialog with a checkbox "include time", a date picker (default today) and, when the checkbox is on, a time picker (default now). On accept the value is inserted at the cursor of the focused editor: `YYYY-MM-DD` or `YYYY-MM-DD HH:mm`. The insertion uses the same mechanism as item 16.


# 18 lazygit

I want version control on the root directory.
one icon in the left toolbar will open a view
into a lazygit interface
or if not easy in js/ts then some alternative
even if jj instead of git

## Status

Deferred on 2026-10-24: the user will decide between lazygit in a terminal and a git pane. The design below is a proposal, not implemented.

## Design

lazygit is a terminal program. Embedding it means a terminal emulator in the renderer (xterm.js) driven by a pseudo-terminal in the main process (node-pty, a native module that must be rebuilt for Electron and allowed in the pnpm build list). That is possible but fragile across the three platforms, and the roadmap allows an alternative.

Decision: a cazalla version-control pane over the `git` command line, in `cazalla/renderer/vcs/`. The main process gets one IPC handler, `mt::cazalla-git`, that runs `git` with given arguments in the content folder and returns stdout, stderr and the exit code (`cazalla/main/git.ts`; registration is one line next to the site handler). The pane shows three parts: the working-tree status (`git status --porcelain=v1`) as a list with checkboxes to stage or unstage files (`git add`, `git restore --staged`), a commit message box with a Commit button (`git commit -m`), and the recent log (`git log --oneline -n 50`). Selecting a status entry shows its diff (`git diff` or `git diff --cached`) as text below. The pane opens from an activity-bar icon as a pane leaf kind `vcs`, and refreshes on the upstream tree events. Commits use the repository's own identity; when none is configured the pane says so. No push or pull in the first version; `jj` is not used.

If the content folder is not a repository, the pane offers "Initialize repository" (`git init`).

# 19 details

## calendar frontmatter

below the calendar
we can hide the frontmatter
since it is not going to change
just keep a heading with the title / date

## alignmentr of math expressions

with $$, they float a bit over the baseline of the normal text
in both reader and editor views of md docs

## Design

The daily-notes pane hides the frontmatter block (`pre.front-matter`) and shows the selected date as a heading above the editor. The frontmatter stays in the file and keeps being rewritten on save.

Inline math: Muya renders `.mu-math` as an inline block with `vertical-align: middle`, which lifts the formula above the text baseline. A cazalla global rule sets `vertical-align: baseline` on `.mu-math` and on its inline render. Reading mode uses the same DOM, so both views are covered.

# 20 for git - superseding 18

We use xterm.js,
an icon in the left tool bar that opens a panel with an term.js interface to lazygit.

## Design

Dependencies: `@xterm/xterm` and `@xterm/addon-fit` in the renderer, `node-pty` in the main process. node-pty is native; `pnpm-workspace.yaml` allows its build and the upstream postinstall rebuilds it for Electron.

`cazalla/main/terminal.ts` keeps one pseudo-terminal per pane: `mt::cazalla-term-open` spawns the program (`lazygit`) in a working directory with the pane's size; output goes to the renderer as `mt::cazalla-term-data`; input, resizes and close come back on three send channels; exit is reported. Registration is one line beside the site handler.

`cazalla/renderer/terminal/TerminalPane.vue` is a pane leaf of kind `terminal` whose path is the working directory. It opens an xterm with the fit addon, relays keystrokes, resizes with its container, and shows the exit code and a Restart button when the program ends. If lazygit cannot be started the error names the fix. The activity-bar git icon opens the pane on the content folder.

Item 18 is superseded by this item.

